const faqItems = [
  {
    question: "Is a block wall always better than a fence?",
    answer:
      "No — it depends on your priorities. A block wall wins on lifespan, sound/wind blocking, and code compliance in canal-adjacent or some subdivision locations. A fence wins on upfront cost and installation speed. Neither is objectively better across every situation.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I mix a fence and a block wall on the same property?",
    answer:
      "Yes, and it's common in Yuma — for example, a masonry wall along a canal-adjacent rear property line (often required by code) paired with standard fencing along the side and front yards where masonry isn't required.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does my HOA require a block wall instead of a fence?",
    answer:
      'Many Yuma-area subdivisions require masonry perimeter fencing sealed with an anti-graffiti coating under City of Yuma Municipal Code § 154-15.06 — check your specific HOA\'s CC&Rs plus the city code before budgeting a fence project. See the <a href="/guides/yuma-fence-wall-permit-rules/">permit &amp; code guide</a>.',
  },
  {
    question: "Which costs less, a fence or a block wall?",
    answer:
      'A fence is almost always less expensive upfront — chain link runs $10–$13/linear ft and vinyl $26–$33/linear ft in Yuma, versus a wall that can run well into five figures for a full perimeter. See the <a href="/costs/fence-cost-yuma-az/">fence cost guide</a> and <a href="/costs/block-wall-cost-yuma-az/">block wall cost guide</a> for the full breakdown.',
  },
];
